knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.path = "",
                      message = FALSE, warning = FALSE)
options(width = 100)
```

*One of this package's seven data sources — one of the two sources that can be imported beside NAR. `vignette("data-sources")` is the overview and puts it in context.*

Statistics Canada's **Road Network File** (product 92-500-X) is a national
street centreline file: 2.25 million segments, 1.17 million kilometres, one
`LINESTRING` each. What makes it an address source rather than a basemap is four
fields — `AFL_VAL`/`ATL_VAL` and `AFR_VAL`/`ATR_VAL`, the civic number range on
the left and right side of each segment.

`rnf_import()` loads it into the same DuckDB file NAR lives in, as its own
tables, and that adds the offline `"rnf"` tier.

```{r connect}
library(cangeocode)
library(dplyr)

con <- nar_connection()
```

```{r import, eval = FALSE}
rnf_import()   # ~340 MB, once
```

## What this adds to the package

**It is the only tier that reaches streets NAR does not carry at all.**

Every other offline tier reads NAR: `"nar"` matches a row, `"nar_interpolate"`
fills a gap between two NAR rows on the same street, `"rqa"` reads Quebec's
register. All of them need the street to exist somewhere in NAR. In the standing
evaluation, **3.7% of filings fail because it does not** — and a street that
exists on the ground is in the road network file whether or not anyone has ever
geocoded a house on it.

Measured on the 379 addresses `geocode()` leaves unplaced out of a 5,000-filing
draw, the tier places **93 of them, 24.5%** — the largest recovery any tier here
has offered, offline or online. Coverage on that draw goes 92.4% → 94.3%. It
costs nothing measurable in time, because it only ever sees what its
predecessors could not place.

## Licence

Statistics Canada Open Licence — the same terms as NAR, and recorded in the
database's own metadata when you import:

```{r licence}
tbl(con, "nar_metadata") |>
  filter(key %in% c("rnf_source", "rnf_licence", "rnf_imported_at")) |>
  collect()
```

## Using it

Add `"rnf"` to `method`. It belongs after the NAR tiers and before the online
ones — it is offline, so it is cheap, but it interpolates where the NAR tiers
match:

```{r tier}
hard <- c("185 Deerfield Rd, Newmarket, ON",
          "1006 Theatre Rd, Gravenhurst, ON",
          "1435 Celebration Dr, Pickering, ON",
          "1545 Maley Dr, Sudbury, ON")

geocode(hard, method = c("nar", "nar_interpolate", "rnf")) |>
  select(input, match_method, n_matches, uncertainty_m, lon, lat)
```

Three of those four are streets NAR has no address on anywhere. The fourth is
the subject of the next section.

## What to watch out for

### It refuses when the answer is ambiguous, and that is the load-bearing part

`1545 Maley Dr` came back `rnf_ambiguous` with `n_matches = 3`: three separate
segments in Sudbury named Maley claim a range containing 1545. The tier does not
pick the shortest, or the nearest to the municipality centroid, or the first. It
declines to answer.

That refusal is where most of the tier's quality comes from. Measured against
NAR on addresses both can place, the unambiguous half sits p50 26 m from NAR's
building point; the ambiguous half carries essentially the whole gross-error
tail. Dropping seven rows to keep ninety-three honest is the trade the tier
makes.

**It is necessary and not sufficient.** Every recovered row that landed more than
2 km from where the filer's own postal code says it should be had
`n_matches == 1` — the refusal never saw them. Two of those three are filers
whose city and postal code name different municipalities, so the check rather
than the tier is wrong. The third is a genuine error, and instructive:
`10272 County Road 2, Cobourg, ON` parses to street `28`, type `HWY`, in
Bailieboro, and RNF placed 10272 on that faithfully. A bad parse, laundered into
a confident coordinate. A tier that exists precisely because NAR does not have
the street cannot sanity-check its input against NAR, so this is the one tier
where a parser error survives all the way to an answer. Roughly 1 row in 93.

### The ranges are partly guesses, and the file will not say which

This is the reason the tier went unbuilt for so long. Statistics Canada's own
documentation says the ranges are observed for some segments, imputed for
others, absent for the rest — and there is no provenance flag in the record
layout. `CLASS` and `RANK` describe the road, not the range. You cannot tell a
surveyed range from an interpolated guess by reading the file.

What replaced the missing flag was a measurement. NAR's building points are an
independent second reading of the same streets, so: how often does a civic
number NAR holds fall inside the range RNF claims for the side the house is
actually on? **89.7%.** That is the honest error rate on the fields, and a floor
on the tier's own. It does not say *which* 10.3% are wrong — but it lets the
tier be priced rather than guessed at.

### Coverage is uneven, and thinnest where NAR is thinnest

13% of segments are unnamed and are not candidates for anything. Of the named
ones, **71.7% carry a range on at least one side**, and the spread is the widest
number in the whole measurement:

| province | of named segments | of all segments |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Nova Scotia, the best | 89.2% | 81.8% |
| Ontario | 81.4% | 75.2% |
| Quebec | 76.2% | 73.5% |
| British Columbia | 66.9% | 59.4% |
| Manitoba | 52.5% | 43.2% |
| Saskatchewan, the worst | 36.6% | 18.7% |

The tier will not serve rural Saskatchewan. That ordering is close enough to
NAR's own that the tier is thinnest exactly where the thing it is compensating
for is thinnest — the two gaps overlap rather than cancelling.

### `uncertainty_m` is a segment-length model, not a measurement

`max(95, 0.35 × len_m)`: 95 m on a short urban block, growing with the segment
for long rural ones. Interpolating a house position from a range assumes the
numbers are spaced evenly along the segment, which they are not, so the error
scales with how far apart the endpoints are. Checked against NAR where both
answer, that interval covers the true distance **91.8%** of the time.

For comparison, interpolation lands p50 24.3 m from NAR's building point and p90
93.3 m — which is about the same accuracy as the difference between a building
point and a blockface point, and rather better than the 95 m floor suggests. The
floor is deliberately pessimistic.

### Accuracy on the overlap does not describe the residual

The p50 26 m above is measured on addresses NAR *also* places, because those are
the only ones there is anything to measure against. The rows the tier exists for
are the ones NAR cannot place, and they are harder. Against the filer's own
postal code, on urban FSAs:

| | n | p50 | within 500 m |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| NAR's answer, on rows the tier also placed | 3,749 | 43 m | 97% |
| the tier, on those same rows | 3,749 | 60 m | 97% |
| **the tier, on rows only it placed** | 46 | **149 m** | **87%** |

Reading down: the tier's own error costs 43 → 60 m, and being an address NAR
could not place costs a further 60 → 149 m. **The residual is harder by more
than twice what the tier's own error is worth.** Whenever a source is validated
where it overlaps NAR, that number is an upper bound on how well it does where
NAR is silent — which is the only place it is being used.

### Take the shapefile

`rnf_import()` downloads the shapefile, and there are two independent reasons,
both of which cost something to rediscover. It is the **only format published
for every release** — the GeoPackage resolves for 2025 alone and 404s on the
archive. And 13 features in the GeoPackage are `CircularString`s, which DuckDB's
spatial extension refuses outright, failing the entire read rather than the
rows. The shapefile format cannot express a CircularString, so those 13 arrive
as ordinary linestrings and no workaround is needed.

```{r disconnect}
DBI::dbDisconnect(con)
```

## Where the measurements live

```{r notes, eval = FALSE}
file.show(system.file("notes", "road-network-file.md", package = "cangeocode"))
```

`data-raw/probe_rnf.R` reproduces every figure above.
